From: Salty <hoffman.viking@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 14:12:35 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] hls: hand out the playlist after the first segment

The controller waited for the client-requested minimum (two or three
segments) before returning the playlist, so playback start paid one or
two extra segment durations while ffmpeg was already producing. Serve a
growing playlist after the first segment instead: hls.js and ExoPlayer
poll the playlist and apply their own buffering either way.
---
 Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs b/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs
index c69040d4..cfc80b2f 100644
--- a/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs
+++ b/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs
@@ -326,6 +326,17 @@ public class DynamicHlsController : BaseJellyfinApiController
                     }
 
                     minSegments = state.MinSegments;
+
+                    // Return the playlist as soon as ONE segment exists instead of the
+                    // client-requested two or three: the player starts fetching (and its
+                    // own buffering logic takes over) one-to-two segment durations sooner,
+                    // and ffmpeg keeps producing regardless. Serving a growing playlist is
+                    // ordinary HLS; hls.js (web/webOS) and ExoPlayer (Fire TV) both poll.
+                    if (minSegments > 1)
+                    {
+                        minSegments = 1;
+                    }
+
                     if (minSegments > 0)
                     {
                         await HlsHelpers.WaitForMinimumSegmentCount(playlistPath, minSegments, _logger, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
-- 
